The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and provides a complete `IFileSystem` interface with a built-in `MockFileSystem` for testing.

Your project targets `net10.0`, so here's what to do:

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI (in `Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

For example, your `ReportGenerator` would become:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        // ...
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        // ...
    }
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.AddDirectory("/tmp/reports");

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(
    mockFs.Directory.GetFiles("/tmp/reports").Single()));
```

---

> **Bonus:** I also noticed `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine` in `ReportGenerator.cs` — those are equally untestable statics. For `DateTime`, .NET 10 has the built-in `TimeProvider` (register `TimeProvider.System` in DI, test with `FakeTimeProvider`). Want me to generate wrappers for those too?
